# OrionLayer v3 — what does not belong in the repository.
#
# The rule is the one the help texts already established: anything
# DERIVED is generated, never committed. Two reasons, and the second
# is the one that matters. Git stores images as whole blobs, not as
# diffs, so every regeneration of an icon set leaves a full extra copy
# in the history forever — and these are exactly the sets that get
# regenerated. And artwork extracted from a copy of Master of Orion 2
# is not ours to redistribute.
#
# Everything ignored here is rebuilt by `python tools/setup.py`, which
# then runs the smoke test to prove the tree is complete.
#
# NOT ignored, deliberately:
#   */assets/**/_src/           HD masters — the input, hand-made
#   icons/_source_sheet.png     the sheet the sidebar icons are cut from
#   nebula/type_*.png           authored HD artwork
#   (nebula_ref/ used to be here — see below, it is ignored now)
#   _black_hole_src.png         the input to make_black_hole_master.py
#   stars/                      36 star sprites. These LOOK derived and
#                               are not: the committed set is trimmed
#                               to content (44 to 206 px, varying),
#                               while make_star_icons.py as it stands
#                               emits uniform 256x256 canvases. Some
#                               earlier version or invocation produced
#                               them and no longer exists. Until the
#                               tool reproduces the artwork the project
#                               was measured against, that artwork is
#                               authored state, not derived state, and
#                               it stays in the repository.

# --- Generated artwork (tools/setup.py rebuilds all of it) ---
# Each of these was checked byte-for-byte: regenerating produces the
# committed file exactly. That check is the whole licence to ignore
# them, and it is why stars/ is NOT in this list — see below.
screens/galaxy_map/assets/ships/*/
!screens/galaxy_map/assets/ships/_src/
screens/galaxy_map/assets/black_hole.png
screens/galaxy_map/assets/icons/*.png
!screens/galaxy_map/assets/icons/_source_sheet.png

# --- Contact sheets (diagnostic output of --sheet) ---
**/_contact_sheet.png

# --- Derived from the user's own MOO2 installation ---
# Extracted by tools/help_extract.py. Not ours to ship.
assets/shared/help/help_*.json

# The original's PRODUCTION NAMES, from the player's own install:
# building names (tools/techname_extract.py, techname.lbx) and
# option strings (tools/estrings_extract.py, estrings.lbx). Both
# feed one column through core/prodname.py and neither is ours.
# The user's own TECHNAME.LBX, so it is theirs and not ours to ship
# (decisions 38, 40, 42); the loader carries a format version and the
# colony screen explains an absent file rather than drawing nothing.
assets/shared/names/

# UNMODIFIED sprites out of STARBG.LBX, used as test fixtures. The
# HD artwork in nebula/ is derived work and stays; this is the
# original, pixel for pixel, and it is the one place in the tree
# that carried it. `tools/nebula_extract.py /path/to/starbg.lbx`
# puts it back. Without it the smoke test cannot check nebula shape
# or brightness — it says so instead of passing quietly.
screens/galaxy_map/assets/nebula_ref/

# UNMODIFIED sprites out of RACEICON.LBX — the population figures the
# colony screens draw, per race and per profession, plus the shared
# native and android. Same rule as nebula_ref/ and the same reason:
# these are the original's own pixels out of somebody's installation,
# not ours to ship. `tools/raceicon_extract.py` puts them back.
# Unlike nebula_ref/ NOTHING IN THE TREE READS THIS ONE — it is a
# reference for a person comparing HD icons against the original, so
# the smoke test neither needs it nor mentions it, and must pass with
# it absent.
screens/colony_summary/assets/raceicon_ref/

# The BASE FIGURE SET the colony screens draw: 54 masters at 28x28,
# cut from the same RACEICON.LBX by the same tool. Decision 50 — the
# shipped product DRAWS the game's own figures; the repository does
# not HOLD them. A mod overrides them one file at a time here, and a
# mod's own figures live in mods/<name>/assets/shared/figures/ and
# are the mod author's to commit.
assets/shared/figures/

# The colony screen's window masks and its built frame plates are
# GONE — Phase B, 12 September 2026, decision 55. Both entries stood
# here until then: the masks were rendered from layout_reference.json
# by tools/frame_mask.py and the plates nine-sliced from the galaxy
# map's master by tools/frame_build.py, both derived under decision 49
# with a byte-for-byte rebuild as the licence. The screen wears one
# fixed image now, screens/colony_summary/assets/frame.png, which is
# AUTHORED artwork and is committed — so there is nothing generated
# left on this screen to ignore, and the tools that made them are
# deleted rather than merely unused.

# --- Local state ---
# settings.json is NOT ignored: it holds window size, skin, language
# and the localhost port, no secrets, and `load_settings()` falls back
# to partial defaults without it — a fresh clone should start in the
# state the project was last known good in, not in a half-default one.
*.log

# --- Python ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

# --- Editors and OS ---
.vscode/
.idea/
*~
*.swp
.DS_Store

# --- Review leftovers ---
# `git show <sha> > diff.txt` and its variants: a diff log is derived
# from the history it was taken out of (decision 40), so it never
# belongs in the tree. Ignored rather than forbidden, because reading
# a commit into a file beside the tree is a reasonable way to review
# one.
diff*.txt

# --- Delivery leftovers (the ZIP era; git replaces all of this) ---
*.zip
tools/tree_manifest.sha256
